window.A.registerBank({"chapter": 14, "title": "Calculus", "syllabusOutcomes": {"14.3": "Derivatives of x^n (any rational n), sin x, cos x, tan x, e^x, ln x — including constant multiples, sums and composite functions (chain rule). Angles always in radians", "14.4": "Differentiate products and quotients", "14.5": "Use differentiation to find gradients, tangents and normals", "14.6": "Use differentiation to find stationary points (points of inflexion excluded)", "14.7": "Apply differentiation to connected rates of change, small increments and approximations", "14.9": "Use the first and second derivative tests to distinguish maxima and minima — full justification expected", "14.11": "Integrate sums of terms in powers of x, including 1/x and 1/(ax+b)", "14.12": "Integrate (ax+b)^n, sin(ax+b), cos(ax+b), sec²(ax+b), e^(ax+b), including n = −1", "14.13": "Evaluate definite integrals and find plane areas: between a line and a curve, between two curves, or a sum of two areas", "14.14": "Apply differentiation and integration to kinematics: displacement, velocity, acceleration in a straight line", "14.15": "Draw and use displacement–time, velocity–time, speed–time and acceleration–time graphs"}, "groups": [{"key": "diff", "label": "Differentiation: chain, product & quotient", "outcomes": ["14.3", "14.4"]}, {"key": "tangent", "label": "Tangents & normals", "outcomes": ["14.5"]}, {"key": "rates", "label": "Rates of change & small increments", "outcomes": ["14.7"]}, {"key": "stat", "label": "Stationary points & second derivatives", "outcomes": ["14.6", "14.9"]}, {"key": "integrate", "label": "Integration techniques", "outcomes": ["14.11", "14.12"]}, {"key": "area", "label": "Areas under and between curves", "outcomes": ["14.13"]}, {"key": "kinematics", "label": "Kinematics", "outcomes": ["14.14", "14.15"]}], "questions": [{"id": "s24-p12-q11", "group": "diff", "source": {"session": "May/June 2024", "paper": "4037/12", "question": "11"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["14.4", "14.10"], "topics": ["product rule with ln", "reverse a derivative to integrate"], "marks": 5, "targetMinutes": 8, "difficulty": 3, "parts": [{"label": "(a)", "text": "Given that \\(y = x^2\\ln x\\), find \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\).", "marks": 2}, {"label": "(b)", "text": "Hence find \\(\\displaystyle\\int x\\ln x\\,dx\\).", "marks": 3}], "statement": "<p><b>(a)</b> Given that \\(\\;y = x^2\\ln x\\), find \\(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\).&emsp;<b>[2]</b></p><p><b>(b)</b> <i>Hence</i> find \\(\\;\\displaystyle\\int x\\ln x\\;\\mathrm{d}x\\).&emsp;<b>[3]</b></p>", "tips": ["(a) <b>Product rule:</b> \\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}(uv) = u'v + uv'\\). With \\(u = x^2\\), \\(v = \\ln x\\): \\(2x\\ln x + x^2\\cdot\\dfrac1x\\).", "(b) \"<b>Hence</b>\" means <i>use part (a)</i> — integration is the reverse of differentiation, so integrating your answer to (a) must give back \\(x^2\\ln x\\).", "So \\(\\displaystyle\\int(2x\\ln x + x)\\,dx = x^2\\ln x\\). Split it: \\(2\\displaystyle\\int x\\ln x\\,dx + \\dfrac{x^2}{2} = x^2\\ln x\\), then rearrange.", "Don't forget \\(+c\\) — every indefinite integral needs the arbitrary constant."], "solution": ["<p><b>(a)</b> \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 2x\\ln x + x^2\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{x} = 2x\\ln x + x\\).</p>", "<p><b>(b)</b> Reversing (a): \\(\\displaystyle\\int(2x\\ln x + x)\\,dx = x^2\\ln x\\).</p>", "<p>\\(2\\displaystyle\\int x\\ln x\\,dx + \\dfrac{x^2}{2} = x^2\\ln x\\)</p>", "<p>\\(\\displaystyle\\int x\\ln x\\,dx = \\dfrac{x^2\\ln x}{2} - \\dfrac{x^2}{4} + c\\).</p>"], "answer": "(a) \\(x + 2x\\ln x\\); (b) \\(\\tfrac12 x^2\\ln x - \\tfrac{x^2}{4} + c\\)", "markScheme": ["(a) 2 — \\(x + 2x\\ln x\\) (M1 for an attempt at the product rule; allow unsimplified for A1)", "(b) M1 — \\(mx^2\\ln x\\) (mark final answer; dep on M1 in part (a))", "(b) A1 — \\(\\frac12 x^2\\ln x\\) oe;  A1 — \\(-\\frac{x^2}{4} + c\\) oe"]}, {"id": "s25-p11-q6a", "group": "diff", "source": {"session": "May/June 2025", "paper": "4037/11", "question": "6(a)"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["14.3", "14.4"], "topics": ["chain rule + quotient rule", "show that"], "marks": 5, "targetMinutes": 8, "difficulty": 3, "statement": "<p>A curve has equation \\(\\;y = \\left(\\dfrac{x^2-1}{x^2+1}\\right)^4\\).</p><p>Show that \\(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\) can be written as \\(\\;\\dfrac{Ax\\left(x^2-1\\right)^3}{\\left(x^2+1\\right)^5}\\), where \\(A\\) is a positive integer to be found.&emsp;<b>[5]</b></p>", "tips": ["This is chain rule <b>wrapped around</b> quotient rule. Outer layer first: \\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}(u^4) = 4u^3\\dfrac{du}{dx}\\) with \\(u = \\dfrac{x^2-1}{x^2+1}\\).", "Inner quotient: \\(\\dfrac{du}{dx} = \\dfrac{2x(x^2+1) - (x^2-1)2x}{(x^2+1)^2}\\). The \\(2x^3\\) terms cancel, leaving \\(\\dfrac{4x}{(x^2+1)^2}\\) — a satisfying simplification worth checking for.", "Multiply: \\(4\\cdot\\dfrac{(x^2-1)^3}{(x^2+1)^3}\\times\\dfrac{4x}{(x^2+1)^2}\\). Add the powers in the denominator: \\(3+2 = 5\\) ✓ matches the target form.", "\"Show that\" — every step must be visible, and finish by stating \\(A = 16\\)."], "solution": ["<p>Let \\(u = \\dfrac{x^2-1}{x^2+1}\\), so \\(y = u^4\\) and \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 4u^3\\dfrac{du}{dx}\\).</p>", "<p>Quotient rule: \\(\\dfrac{du}{dx} = \\dfrac{2x(x^2+1) - (x^2-1)(2x)}{(x^2+1)^2} = \\dfrac{2x^3+2x-2x^3+2x}{(x^2+1)^2} = \\dfrac{4x}{(x^2+1)^2}\\).</p>", "<p>\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 4\\left(\\dfrac{x^2-1}{x^2+1}\\right)^3\\times\\dfrac{4x}{(x^2+1)^2} = \\dfrac{16x(x^2-1)^3}{(x^2+1)^{3+2}} = \\dfrac{16x(x^2-1)^3}{(x^2+1)^5}\\).</p>", "<p>So \\(A = 16\\). ✓</p>"], "answer": "\\(A = 16\\)", "markScheme": ["M1 — chain rule structure: \\(4\\left(\\frac{x^2-1}{x^2+1}\\right)^3\\times\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\frac{x^2-1}{x^2+1}\\right)\\)", "M1 — quotient rule attempt on the inner fraction with the correct structure", "A1 — inner derivative \\(\\frac{4x}{(x^2+1)^2}\\) correctly simplified", "M1 — combines and adds the indices in the denominator (dep)", "A1 — \\(\\frac{16x(x^2-1)^3}{(x^2+1)^5}\\) with \\(A = 16\\) stated (AG)"]}, {"id": "s25-p12-q8", "group": "diff", "source": {"session": "May/June 2025", "paper": "4037/12", "question": "8"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["14.4", "14.7"], "alsoChapters": [6], "topics": ["quotient rule with ln", "answer as a single log", "small change"], "marks": 6, "targetMinutes": 9, "difficulty": 3, "parts": [{"label": "(a)", "text": "It is given that \\(y = \\dfrac{\\ln(3x^2+16)}{x+2}\\). Find \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\) when x = 0. Give your answer in the form \\(\\ln p\\), where p is a constant.", "marks": 5}, {"label": "(b)", "text": "Given that x increases from 0 to h, where h is small, write down the approximate change in y.", "marks": 1}], "statement": "<p>It is given that \\(\\;y = \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(3x^2+16\\right)}{x+2}\\).</p><p><b>(a)</b> Find \\(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\) when \\(x = 0\\). Give your answer in the form \\(\\ln p\\), where \\(p\\) is a constant.&emsp;<b>[5]</b></p><p><b>(b)</b> Given that \\(x\\) increases from 0 to \\(h\\), where \\(h\\) is small, write down the approximate change in \\(y\\).&emsp;<b>[1]</b></p>", "tips": ["<b>Quotient rule:</b> \\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(\\dfrac{u}{v}\\right) = \\dfrac{u'v - uv'}{v^2}\\) — numerator derivative FIRST; getting the order backwards flips the sign.", "\\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\ln(3x^2+16) = \\dfrac{6x}{3x^2+16}\\) (chain rule: derivative of the inside over the inside).", "At \\(x=0\\) the first term vanishes (because of the \\(6x\\)), leaving \\(\\dfrac{-\\ln16}{4}\\).", "\"In the form \\(\\ln p\\)\": use the power law backwards — \\(-\\tfrac14\\ln16 = \\ln 16^{-1/4} = \\ln\\tfrac12\\).", "(b) <b>Small-change formula:</b> \\(\\delta y \\approx \\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\times\\delta x\\). Here \\(\\delta x = h\\), so \\(\\delta y \\approx h\\ln\\tfrac12\\) — write \\(h\\) times your answer, not the other way round in a confusing form."], "solution": ["<p><b>(a)</b> \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = \\dfrac{(x+2)\\cdot\\dfrac{6x}{3x^2+16} - \\ln(3x^2+16)}{(x+2)^2}\\).</p>", "<p>At \\(x=0\\): numerator \\(= 2\\times0 - \\ln16 = -\\ln16\\); denominator \\(= 4\\).</p>", "<p>\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = -\\dfrac{\\ln16}{4} = -\\tfrac14\\ln16 = \\ln\\left(16^{-1/4}\\right) = \\ln\\tfrac12\\).</p>", "<p><b>(b)</b> \\(\\delta y \\approx \\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\times\\delta x = h\\ln\\tfrac12\\).</p>"], "answer": "(a) \\(\\ln\\tfrac12\\); (b) \\(h\\ln\\tfrac12\\)", "markScheme": ["(a) B1 — \\(\\frac{6x}{3x^2+16}\\); M1 — differentiation of a quotient (or equivalent product) with correct structure; A1 — all other terms correct apart from \\(\\frac{6x}{3x^2+16}\\)", "(a) M1 dep — substitutes \\(x=0\\) and obtains a single log term; A1 — \\(\\ln\\frac12\\) from correct work (all previous marks must be awarded)", "(b) B1 — Change \\(= h\\ln\\frac12\\) oe FT on their single-log answer (B0 for \\(\\ln\\frac12 h\\))"]}, {"id": "s24-p22-q5", "group": "tangent", "source": {"session": "May/June 2024", "paper": "4037/22", "question": "5"}, "calc": true, "outcomes": ["14.3", "14.5"], "topics": ["differentiate e^(ax+b)", "tangent in exact form", "axis intercept"], "marks": 6, "targetMinutes": 9, "difficulty": 2, "statement": "<p>A curve has equation \\(\\;y = 5\\mathrm{e}^{2x-1} + \\mathrm{e}\\). The tangent to the curve at the point where \\(x = 1\\) cuts the \\(x\\)-axis at the point \\(P\\).</p><p>Find the equation of the tangent in the form \\(y = mx+c\\), where \\(m\\) and \\(c\\) are exact values, and hence find the \\(x\\)-coordinate of \\(P\\).&emsp;<b>[6]</b></p>", "tips": ["\\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\mathrm{e}^{ax+b} = a\\,\\mathrm{e}^{ax+b}\\) — multiply by the derivative of the exponent. Note \\(+\\mathrm{e}\\) is a CONSTANT (e ≈ 2.718), so it differentiates to 0.", "Two things at \\(x=1\\): the gradient \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 10\\mathrm{e}^{1} = 10\\mathrm{e}\\), and the point \\(y = 5\\mathrm{e} + \\mathrm{e} = 6\\mathrm{e}\\).", "Tangent: \\(y - y_1 = m(x-x_1)\\) → \\(y - 6\\mathrm{e} = 10\\mathrm{e}(x-1)\\). Expand to \\(y = mx+c\\) form keeping \\(\\mathrm{e}\\) exact.", "\"Cuts the \\(x\\)-axis\" means \\(y = 0\\): \\(0 = 10\\mathrm{e}x - 4\\mathrm{e}\\). The \\(\\mathrm{e}\\) divides out — a neat check that your algebra is right."], "solution": ["<p>\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 10\\mathrm{e}^{2x-1}\\) (the \\(+\\mathrm{e}\\) is constant).</p>", "<p>At \\(x=1\\): gradient \\(= 10\\mathrm{e}^{1} = 10\\mathrm{e}\\), and \\(y = 5\\mathrm{e}^{1} + \\mathrm{e} = 6\\mathrm{e}\\).</p>", "<p>Tangent: \\(y - 6\\mathrm{e} = 10\\mathrm{e}(x - 1) \\Rightarrow y = 10\\mathrm{e}x - 10\\mathrm{e} + 6\\mathrm{e} = 10\\mathrm{e}x - 4\\mathrm{e}\\).</p>", "<p>At \\(P\\), \\(y=0\\): \\(10\\mathrm{e}x = 4\\mathrm{e} \\Rightarrow x = \\dfrac{4}{10} = 0.4\\).</p>"], "answer": "\\(y = 10\\mathrm{e}x - 4\\mathrm{e}\\); \\(P\\) has \\(x = 0.4\\)", "markScheme": ["M2 — \\(\\frac{dy}{dx} = 10\\mathrm{e}^{2x-1}\\,[+0]\\) oe (M1 for \\(k\\mathrm{e}^{2x-1}\\), \\(k\\neq10\\); SCM1 for \\(10\\mathrm{e}^{2x-1}+c\\))", "A1 — \\(\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{x=1} = 10\\mathrm{e}\\) and \\(y = 6\\mathrm{e}\\) (FT their derivative)", "M1 — \\(y - 6\\mathrm{e} = 10\\mathrm{e}(x-1)\\) oe (FT their gradient and y)", "A1 — \\(y = 10\\mathrm{e}x - 4\\mathrm{e}\\) isw", "A1 — \\(x\\)-coordinate of \\(P\\) = 0.4 oe isw (dep on a correct tangent with exact values)"]}, {"id": "w24-p23-q5", "group": "tangent", "source": {"session": "Oct/Nov 2024", "paper": "4037/23", "question": "5"}, "calc": true, "outcomes": ["14.4", "14.5"], "alsoChapters": [5], "topics": ["quotient rule with a surd", "tangent meets a line"], "marks": 8, "targetMinutes": 12, "difficulty": 3, "statement": "<p>The tangent to the curve \\(\\;y = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+1}}{x}\\;\\) at the point where \\(x = 3\\) meets the line \\(\\;y = x-16\\;\\) at the point \\(A\\).</p><p>Find the coordinates of \\(A\\).&emsp;<b>[8]</b></p>", "tips": ["Write the surd as a power first: \\(y = \\dfrac{(x+1)^{1/2}}{x}\\). Then quotient rule with \\(u = (x+1)^{1/2}\\), \\(u' = \\tfrac12(x+1)^{-1/2}\\).", "At \\(x=3\\): \\(y = \\dfrac{\\sqrt4}{3} = \\dfrac23\\) — find the point as well as the gradient.", "Gradient at \\(x=3\\): \\(\\dfrac{3\\cdot\\tfrac12(4)^{-1/2} - \\sqrt4}{9} = \\dfrac{0.75 - 2}{9} = -\\dfrac{5}{36}\\). Keep it as an exact fraction.", "Then <b>solve simultaneously</b> with \\(y = x-16\\) — the last two marks are for the intersection, so leave time for it."], "solution": ["<p>\\(y = (x+1)^{1/2}x^{-1}\\). Quotient rule: \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = \\dfrac{x\\cdot\\tfrac12(x+1)^{-1/2} - (x+1)^{1/2}}{x^2}\\).</p>", "<p>At \\(x=3\\): \\(y = \\dfrac{2}{3}\\); \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = \\dfrac{3\\times\\tfrac12\\times\\tfrac12 - 2}{9} = \\dfrac{0.75-2}{9} = -\\dfrac{5}{36}\\).</p>", "<p>Tangent: \\(y - \\dfrac23 = -\\dfrac{5}{36}(x-3)\\), i.e. \\(y = -\\dfrac{5}{36}x + \\dfrac{13}{12}\\).</p>", "<p>Meets \\(y = x-16\\): \\(x - 16 = -\\dfrac{5}{36}x + \\dfrac{13}{12} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{41}{36}x = \\dfrac{205}{12} \\Rightarrow x = 15\\).</p>", "<p>Then \\(y = 15-16 = -1\\). So \\(A(15, -1)\\).</p>"], "answer": "\\(A(15, -1)\\)", "markScheme": ["B1 — \\(y = \\frac23\\) when \\(x=3\\) soi", "M2 — correct derivative \\(\\frac{x\\times\\frac12(x+1)^{-1/2} - \\sqrt{x+1}}{x^2}\\) oe (M1 for an attempt at the quotient rule)", "M1 dep — substitutes \\(x=3\\) into a derivative including \\((x+1)^{-1/2}\\)", "A1 dep — \\(-\\frac{5}{36}\\) isw (dep on a correct derivative)", "A1 — \\(y = -\\frac{5}{36}x + \\frac{13}{12}\\) oe soi (FT their non-zero \\(\\frac23\\) and \\(-\\frac{5}{36}\\))", "B2 — \\(A(15,-1)\\) (dep on all previous marks; B1 dep for \\(x=15\\) or \\(y=-1\\))"]}, {"id": "w24-p12-q7", "group": "rates", "source": {"session": "Oct/Nov 2024", "paper": "4037/12", "question": "7"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["14.4", "14.7"], "alsoChapters": [6], "topics": ["quotient rule with ln", "connected rates of change", "exact form"], "marks": 6, "targetMinutes": 9, "difficulty": 3, "statement": "<p>It is given that \\(\\;y = \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(3x^2-1\\right)}{x+2}\\), for \\(x > \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\).</p><p>When \\(x = 1\\), \\(y\\) is increasing at the rate of \\(h\\) units per second. Find, in terms of \\(h\\), the corresponding rate of change in \\(x\\), giving your answer in exact form.&emsp;<b>[6]</b></p>", "tips": ["<b>Connected rates use the chain rule:</b> \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt} = \\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\times\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\). You are given \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt} = h\\) and want \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\), so <b>divide</b>: \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt} = \\dfrac{dy}{dt}\\div\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\).", "Quotient rule: \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = \\dfrac{(x+2)\\dfrac{6x}{3x^2-1} - \\ln(3x^2-1)}{(x+2)^2}\\).", "At \\(x=1\\): \\(3x^2-1 = 2\\), so \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = \\dfrac{3\\times3 - \\ln2}{9} = \\dfrac{9-\\ln2}{9}\\).", "Then \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt} = h \\div \\dfrac{9-\\ln2}{9} = \\dfrac{9h}{9-\\ln2}\\). \"Exact form\" → keep \\(\\ln2\\), and note the mark scheme awards A0 for small-changes notation here (this is a RATE, not an increment)."], "solution": ["<p>\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = \\dfrac{(x+2)\\cdot\\dfrac{6x}{3x^2-1} - \\ln(3x^2-1)}{(x+2)^2}\\).</p>", "<p>At \\(x = 1\\): \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = \\dfrac{3\\times\\dfrac{6}{2} - \\ln2}{3^2} = \\dfrac{9 - \\ln2}{9}\\).</p>", "<p>Chain rule: \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt} = \\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\times\\dfrac{dx}{dt} \\Rightarrow h = \\dfrac{9-\\ln2}{9}\\times\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\).</p>", "<p>\\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt} = \\dfrac{9h}{9-\\ln2}\\) units per second.</p>"], "answer": "\\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt} = \\dfrac{9h}{9-\\ln 2}\\)", "markScheme": ["B1 — \\(\\frac{6x}{3x^2-1}\\)", "M1 — correct attempt at differentiating a quotient (or a correct product form)", "A1 — all terms correct apart from \\(\\frac{6x}{3x^2-1}\\)", "M1 — uses \\(x = 1\\) in their \\(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\): \\(\\frac{9-\\ln2}{9}\\) (must see the substitution unless 0.923 comes from a correct derivative)", "M1 — \\(\\frac{h}{their\\left(\\frac{9-\\ln2}{9}\\right)}\\) with \\(x=1\\) substituted; A1 — \\(\\frac{9h}{9-\\ln2}\\) or exact equivalent (A0 if small-changes notation is used)"]}, {"id": "s25-p21-q5", "group": "rates", "source": {"session": "May/June 2025", "paper": "4037/21", "question": "5"}, "calc": true, "outcomes": ["14.3", "14.4", "14.7"], "topics": ["product rule with tan", "small increment"], "marks": 4, "targetMinutes": 6, "difficulty": 2, "statement": "<p>Given that \\(\\;y = x^2\\tan\\dfrac{x}{2}\\), use calculus to find the approximate change in \\(y\\) as \\(x\\) increases from \\(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\) to \\(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}+h\\), where \\(h\\) is small.&emsp;<b>[4]</b></p>", "tips": ["\\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\tan(ax) = a\\sec^2(ax)\\), so \\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\tan\\dfrac{x}{2} = \\dfrac12\\sec^2\\dfrac{x}{2}\\).", "Product rule: \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 2x\\tan\\dfrac{x}{2} + x^2\\cdot\\dfrac12\\sec^2\\dfrac{x}{2}\\).", "<b>Radians!</b> Angles in calculus are always in radians — set your calculator to RAD before evaluating at \\(x = \\pi/3\\).", "\\(\\delta y \\approx \\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\times h\\). Evaluate the derivative at \\(\\pi/3\\) (≈ 1.94) and write the answer as \\(1.94h\\)."], "solution": ["<p>\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 2x\\tan\\dfrac{x}{2} + \\dfrac{x^2}{2}\\sec^2\\dfrac{x}{2}\\).</p>", "<p>At \\(x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\): \\(\\tan\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\), \\(\\sec^2\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos^2(\\pi/6)} = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\).</p>", "<p>\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\times\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3} + \\dfrac{\\pi^2}{18}\\times\\dfrac43 = 1.209 + 0.731 = 1.940\\).</p>", "<p>\\(\\delta y \\approx \\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\times h = 1.94h\\).</p>"], "answer": "\\(\\delta y \\approx 1.94h\\)", "markScheme": ["2 — \\(\\frac{dy}{dx} = 2x\\tan\\frac x2 + \\frac{x^2}{2}\\sec^2\\frac x2\\) oe (B1 for \\(\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\tan\\frac x2\\right) = \\frac12\\sec^2\\frac x2\\) soi, or M1 FT for a correct product-rule structure)", "M1 — correct small-changes relationship: \\(\\frac{\\delta y}{h} = \\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{x=\\pi/3}\\) or \\(\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{x=\\pi/3}\\times h\\)", "A1 — \\(1.94h\\) (dep on B2)"]}, {"id": "s24-p21-q6", "group": "rates", "source": {"session": "May/June 2024", "paper": "4037/21", "question": "6"}, "calc": true, "outcomes": ["14.3", "14.4", "14.7"], "topics": ["product rule with trig powers", "approximate change"], "marks": 5, "targetMinutes": 8, "difficulty": 3, "statement": "<p>Variables \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are such that \\(\\;y = \\cos x\\sin^2 x\\).</p><p>Use differentiation to find the approximate change in \\(y\\) as \\(x\\) increases from 3 to \\(3+h\\), where \\(h\\) is small.&emsp;<b>[5]</b></p>", "tips": ["Two layers here: \\(\\sin^2x\\) needs the CHAIN rule — \\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}(\\sin^2x) = 2\\sin x\\cos x\\) — and then the whole thing needs the PRODUCT rule.", "\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = \\cos x(2\\sin x\\cos x) + (-\\sin x)\\sin^2 x = 2\\sin x\\cos^2x - \\sin^3x\\).", "The \"3\" is <b>3 radians</b>, not 3°. Calculator in RAD mode.", "\\(\\delta y \\approx \\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\times h\\): evaluate \\(2\\sin3\\cos^23 - \\sin^33 \\approx 0.2738\\), so \\(\\delta y \\approx 0.274h\\). Keep 4+ significant figures in the coefficient before rounding."], "solution": ["<p>Chain rule inside: \\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}(\\sin^2x) = 2\\sin x\\cos x\\).</p>", "<p>Product rule: \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = \\cos x(2\\sin x\\cos x) + (-\\sin x)(\\sin^2 x) = 2\\sin x\\cos^2x - \\sin^3x\\).</p>", "<p>At \\(x = 3\\) (radians): \\(\\sin3 = 0.14112\\), \\(\\cos3 = -0.98999\\).</p>", "<p>\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 2(0.14112)(0.98009) - (0.14112)^3 = 0.27662 - 0.00281 = 0.27381\\).</p>", "<p>\\(\\delta y \\approx 0.274h\\).</p>"], "answer": "\\(\\delta y \\approx 0.274h\\) (0.2738…h)", "markScheme": ["B1 — \\(\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin^2x) = 2\\sin x\\cos x\\) soi", "M1 — product rule structure: \\(\\cos x\\times their(2\\sin x\\cos x) + (their -\\sin x)\\times\\sin^2x\\)", "A1 — correct derivative isw (FT their \\(\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin^2x)\\))", "M1 — \\(\\frac{\\delta y}{h} = their(2\\sin3\\cos^23 - \\sin^33)\\) or better (FT their derivative)", "A1 — \\(0.274h\\) or \\(0.2738[08\\ldots]h\\) with the coefficient to 4+ sf (dep on a correct derivative)"]}, {"id": "s24-p21-q7", "group": "stat", "source": {"session": "May/June 2024", "paper": "4037/21", "question": "7"}, "calc": true, "outcomes": ["14.2", "14.3"], "topics": ["second derivative", "identity true for all x", "comparing coefficients"], "marks": 4, "targetMinutes": 6, "difficulty": 3, "statement": "<p>It is given that \\(\\;y = mx^2 + \\dfrac{x}{2} + n\\), where \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) are non-zero constants. It is also given that</p><p style=\"text-align:center\">\\(3\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2}\\right) = \\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\right)^2 - y\\)</p><p>for <b>all</b> values of \\(x\\). Find the values of \\(m\\) and \\(n\\).&emsp;<b>[4]</b></p>", "tips": ["Differentiate twice: \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 2mx + \\dfrac12\\) and \\(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2} = 2m\\) (a constant — the \\(x\\) has gone).", "Substitute into the identity and expand \\(\\left(2mx+\\tfrac12\\right)^2 = 4m^2x^2 + 2mx + \\tfrac14\\).", "<b>\"For all values of x\" is the key phrase</b> — it means the two sides are identical polynomials, so you can compare coefficients of \\(x^2\\), \\(x\\) and the constant separately.", "\\(x^2\\): \\(0 = 4m^2 - m \\Rightarrow m = \\tfrac14\\) (as \\(m\\neq0\\)). Constant: \\(6m = \\tfrac14 - n \\Rightarrow n = -\\tfrac54\\). Check the \\(x\\) coefficient is consistent (it is)."], "solution": ["<p>\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 2mx + \\dfrac12\\), &ensp;\\(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2} = 2m\\).</p>", "<p>Substitute: \\(3(2m) = \\left(2mx+\\tfrac12\\right)^2 - \\left(mx^2 + \\tfrac{x}{2} + n\\right)\\)</p>", "<p>\\(6m = 4m^2x^2 + 2mx + \\tfrac14 - mx^2 - \\tfrac{x}{2} - n\\)</p>", "<p>Comparing \\(x^2\\): \\(0 = 4m^2 - m = m(4m-1)\\); since \\(m\\neq0\\), \\(m = \\tfrac14\\).</p>", "<p>Comparing \\(x\\): \\(0 = 2m - \\tfrac12 = \\tfrac12-\\tfrac12 = 0\\) ✓ (consistent).</p>", "<p>Comparing constants: \\(6m = \\tfrac14 - n \\Rightarrow \\tfrac32 = \\tfrac14 - n \\Rightarrow n = -\\tfrac54\\).</p>"], "answer": "\\(m = \\tfrac14,\\quad n = -\\tfrac54\\)", "markScheme": ["B1 — \\(\\frac{dy}{dx} = 2mx + \\frac12\\)", "B1 — \\(\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = 2m\\)", "2 — \\(m = \\frac14\\) and \\(n = -\\frac54\\) and no other values (M1 for \\(3(2m) = \\left(2mx+\\frac12\\right)^2 - \\left(mx^2+\\frac x2+n\\right)\\) soi)"]}, {"id": "s25-p12-q12", "group": "stat", "source": {"session": "May/June 2025", "paper": "4037/12", "question": "12"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["14.6", "14.12"], "topics": ["integrate to recover the curve", "constant of integration from a point", "stationary point"], "marks": 6, "targetMinutes": 9, "difficulty": 3, "statement": "<p>A curve is such that its gradient at the point \\((x, y)\\) is given by \\(\\;(5x-2)^{\\frac13}\\).</p><p>The curve passes through the point \\(\\left(2, \\dfrac{32}{5}\\right)\\).</p><p>Find the coordinates of the stationary point on the curve.&emsp;<b>[6]</b></p>", "tips": ["<b>Stationary point first:</b> gradient \\(= 0\\) when \\((5x-2)^{1/3} = 0 \\Rightarrow x = \\tfrac25\\). This must come from the FIRST derivative (you are given \\(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\), not \\(y\\)).", "To get the \\(y\\)-coordinate you need the curve, so <b>integrate</b>: \\(\\displaystyle\\int(5x-2)^{1/3}dx = \\dfrac{(5x-2)^{4/3}}{\\tfrac43\\times5} = \\dfrac{3}{20}(5x-2)^{4/3} + c\\).", "Rule for \\((ax+b)^n\\): raise the power by 1, divide by the new power AND by \\(a\\).", "Use the given point to find \\(c\\): at \\(x=2\\), \\((5x-2) = 8\\) and \\(8^{4/3} = 16\\), so \\(\\tfrac{32}{5} = \\tfrac{3}{20}(16) + c = \\tfrac{12}{5}+c \\Rightarrow c = 4\\). Then at \\(x=\\tfrac25\\) the bracket is 0, so \\(y = c = 4\\)."], "solution": ["<p>Stationary point: \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = (5x-2)^{1/3} = 0 \\Rightarrow x = \\dfrac25\\).</p>", "<p>Integrate: \\(y = \\displaystyle\\int(5x-2)^{1/3}dx = \\dfrac{(5x-2)^{4/3}}{\\frac43\\times5} + c = \\dfrac{3}{20}(5x-2)^{4/3} + c\\).</p>", "<p>Through \\(\\left(2,\\tfrac{32}{5}\\right)\\): \\(\\dfrac{32}{5} = \\dfrac{3}{20}\\big((5\\times2)-2\\big)^{4/3} + c = \\dfrac{3}{20}(8)^{4/3} + c = \\dfrac{3}{20}(16)+c = \\dfrac{12}{5}+c\\).</p>", "<p>\\(c = \\dfrac{32}{5} - \\dfrac{12}{5} = 4\\).</p>", "<p>At \\(x = \\tfrac25\\): \\(y = \\dfrac{3}{20}(0)^{4/3} + 4 = 4\\). Stationary point \\(\\left(\\dfrac25, 4\\right)\\).</p>"], "answer": "\\(\\left(\\tfrac25,\\ 4\\right)\\)", "markScheme": ["B1 — when \\(\\frac{dy}{dx}=0\\), \\(x = \\frac25\\) (must come from the first derivative, not the second)", "2 — \\(y = \\frac{3}{20}(5x-2)^{4/3}\\,(+c)\\) (M1 for \\(k(5x-2)^{4/3}\\))", "M1 dep — attempt to find \\(c\\) using their \\(k(5x-2)^{4/3}\\) and the given point: \\(\\frac{32}{5} = \\frac{3}{20}\\big((5\\times2)-2\\big)^{4/3}+c\\)", "A1 — \\(c = 4\\) (allow an unsimplified fraction)", "B1 dep — stationary point \\(\\left(\\frac25, 4\\right)\\) (dep on the first B mark)"]}, {"id": "s24-p11-q6", "group": "integrate", "source": {"session": "May/June 2024", "paper": "4037/11", "question": "6"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["14.11", "14.12", "14.13"], "topics": ["1/(ax+b) integrates to ln", "(ax+b)^(-2)", "definite integral in exact form"], "marks": 4, "targetMinutes": 6, "difficulty": 2, "statement": "<p>Find \\(\\;\\displaystyle\\int_{2}^{4}\\left(\\dfrac{2}{2x-3} - \\dfrac{3}{(3x-5)^2}\\right)\\mathrm{d}x\\), giving your answer in exact form.&emsp;<b>[4]</b></p>", "tips": ["Two standard forms. \\(\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{a}{ax+b}dx = \\ln(ax+b)\\) — here the numerator 2 exactly matches the \\(2x\\), so it integrates to \\(\\ln(2x-3)\\) with no extra fraction.", "For \\(\\dfrac{3}{(3x-5)^2}\\), write it as \\(3(3x-5)^{-2}\\): raising the power gives \\(\\dfrac{3(3x-5)^{-1}}{(-1)(3)} = -\\dfrac{1}{3x-5}\\). With the minus sign in front, this becomes \\(+\\dfrac{1}{3x-5}\\).", "So the antiderivative is \\(\\ln(2x-3) + \\dfrac{1}{3x-5}\\). Substitute the limits — no \\(+c\\) needed for a definite integral.", "\"Exact form\" → leave \\(\\ln5\\) and the fraction: at \\(x=4\\) you get \\(\\ln5+\\tfrac17\\), at \\(x=2\\) you get \\(\\ln1+1 = 1\\)."], "solution": ["<p>\\(\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{2}{2x-3}dx = \\ln(2x-3)\\); &ensp;\\(\\displaystyle\\int -3(3x-5)^{-2}dx = \\dfrac{-3(3x-5)^{-1}}{-1\\times3} = \\dfrac{1}{3x-5}\\).</p>", "<p>So \\(\\displaystyle\\int_2^4 = \\left[\\ln(2x-3) + \\dfrac{1}{3x-5}\\right]_2^4\\).</p>", "<p>At \\(x=4\\): \\(\\ln5 + \\dfrac17\\). &ensp;At \\(x=2\\): \\(\\ln1 + \\dfrac11 = 0 + 1 = 1\\).</p>", "<p>Value \\(= \\ln5 + \\dfrac17 - 1 = \\ln5 - \\dfrac67\\).</p>"], "answer": "\\(\\ln 5 - \\dfrac67\\)", "markScheme": ["B1 — \\(\\ln(2x-3)\\) (allow unsimplified)", "B1 — \\(\\frac{1}{3x-5}\\) oe", "M1 — correct application of the limits in their integral; must be of the form \\(a\\ln(2x-3) \\pm \\frac{b}{3x-5}\\): \\(\\ln5 + \\frac17 - 1\\)", "A1 — \\(\\ln5 - \\frac67\\) cao"]}, {"id": "w24-p23-q6", "group": "integrate", "source": {"session": "Oct/Nov 2024", "paper": "4037/23", "question": "6"}, "calc": true, "outcomes": ["14.12", "14.13"], "topics": ["(ax+b)^(-1/2)", "e^(ax+b) with an algebraic limit"], "marks": 5, "targetMinutes": 8, "difficulty": 2, "parts": [{"label": "(a)", "text": "Find \\(\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}}\\,dx\\).", "marks": 2}, {"label": "(b)", "text": "Find, in terms of a, \\(\\displaystyle\\int_{0.5}^{a}\\mathrm{e}^{(1-2x)}\\,dx\\).", "marks": 3}], "statement": "<p><b>(a)</b> Find \\(\\;\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}}\\;\\mathrm{d}x\\).&emsp;<b>[2]</b></p><p><b>(b)</b> Find, in terms of \\(a\\), \\(\\;\\displaystyle\\int_{0.5}^{a}\\mathrm{e}^{(1-2x)}\\;\\mathrm{d}x\\).&emsp;<b>[3]</b></p>", "tips": ["(a) Rewrite the surd as a power: \\(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}} = (3x+2)^{-1/2}\\). Then add 1 to the power (\\(\\to \\tfrac12\\)) and divide by the new power AND by 3.", "(a) \\(\\dfrac{(3x+2)^{1/2}}{\\tfrac12\\times3} = \\dfrac{2}{3}(3x+2)^{1/2} + c\\). Indefinite → <b>\\(+c\\) required</b>.", "(b) \\(\\displaystyle\\int \\mathrm{e}^{ax+b}dx = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{e}^{ax+b}}{a}\\). Here \\(a = -2\\), so you get \\(-\\tfrac12\\mathrm{e}^{1-2x}\\).", "(b) Substituting the lower limit \\(x = 0.5\\) makes the exponent \\(1-1 = 0\\), so \\(\\mathrm{e}^0 = 1\\) — that is why the answer has a clean \\(+\\tfrac12\\)."], "solution": ["<p><b>(a)</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int(3x+2)^{-1/2}dx = \\dfrac{(3x+2)^{1/2}}{\\tfrac12\\times3} + c = \\dfrac{2}{3}(3x+2)^{1/2} + c\\).</p>", "<p><b>(b)</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int\\mathrm{e}^{1-2x}dx = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathrm{e}^{1-2x}\\).</p>", "<p>\\(\\left[-\\dfrac{\\mathrm{e}^{1-2x}}{2}\\right]_{0.5}^{a} = -\\dfrac{\\mathrm{e}^{1-2a}}{2} - \\left(-\\dfrac{\\mathrm{e}^{0}}{2}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\mathrm{e}^{1-2a}}{2} + \\dfrac12\\).</p>"], "answer": "(a) \\(\\tfrac23(3x+2)^{1/2}+c\\); (b) \\(-\\dfrac{\\mathrm{e}^{1-2a}}{2}+\\dfrac12\\)", "markScheme": ["(a) 2 — \\(\\frac23(3x+2)^{1/2}(+c)\\) oe (B1 for \\(k(3x+2)^{1/2}\\) soi, \\(k\\) a non-zero constant)", "(b) 3 — \\(-\\frac{\\mathrm{e}^{1-2a}}{2} + \\frac12\\) oe isw (B2 for \\(-\\frac12\\mathrm{e}^{(1-2x)}\\) oe; B1 for \\(k\\mathrm{e}^{(1-2x)}\\) or \\(k\\mathrm{e}^{(1-2a)}\\), \\(k\\) a non-zero constant)"]}, {"id": "w25-p13-q8", "group": "area", "source": {"session": "Oct/Nov 2025", "paper": "4037/13", "question": "8"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["14.13"], "alsoChapters": [4], "topics": ["area between two curves", "quartic intersection via a quadratic in x²"], "marks": 7, "targetMinutes": 11, "difficulty": 3, "statement": "<p>The diagram shows part of each of the curves \\(\\;y = 12-x^2\\;\\) and \\(\\;y = x^4-4x^2+8\\).</p><p>Find the area of the shaded region enclosed by the two curves.&emsp;<b>[7]</b></p>", "figure": {"x": [-3, 3], "y": [-1, 14], "xTicks": 1, "yTicks": 2, "curves": [{"poly": [-1, 0, 12]}, {"poly": [1, 0, -4, 0, 8], "cls": "c1"}], "points": [[-2, 8, ""], [2, 8, ""]]}, "tips": ["<b>Limits first:</b> the curves meet where \\(12-x^2 = x^4-4x^2+8\\), i.e. \\(x^4-3x^2-4 = 0\\).", "That is a quadratic in \\(x^2\\): \\((x^2-4)(x^2+1) = 0\\). Since \\(x^2 = -1\\) has no real solutions, \\(x = \\pm2\\) only.", "<b>Area between curves = ∫(top − bottom) dx.</b> Subtract BEFORE integrating — it is much less work than two separate integrals: \\((12-x^2) - (x^4-4x^2+8) = -x^4+3x^2+4\\).", "Integrate between \\(-2\\) and \\(2\\). (Symmetry shortcut: integrate 0 to 2 and double — the mark scheme allows it.) Answer \\(\\tfrac{96}{5}\\)."], "solution": ["<p>Intersections: \\(12-x^2 = x^4-4x^2+8 \\Rightarrow x^4 - 3x^2 - 4 = 0\\).</p>", "<p>\\((x^2-4)(x^2+1) = 0 \\Rightarrow x^2 = 4\\) (as \\(x^2=-1\\) has no real roots) \\(\\Rightarrow x = \\pm2\\).</p>", "<p>Area \\(= \\displaystyle\\int_{-2}^{2}\\big[(12-x^2) - (x^4-4x^2+8)\\big]dx = \\int_{-2}^{2}(-x^4+3x^2+4)\\,dx\\).</p>", "<p>\\(= \\left[-\\dfrac{x^5}{5} + x^3 + 4x\\right]_{-2}^{2} = \\left(-\\dfrac{32}{5}+8+8\\right) - \\left(\\dfrac{32}{5}-8-8\\right)\\)</p>", "<p>\\(= \\left(-\\dfrac{32}{5}+16\\right) + \\left(-\\dfrac{32}{5}+16\\right) = 32 - \\dfrac{64}{5} = \\dfrac{96}{5} = 19.2\\).</p>"], "answer": "\\(\\dfrac{96}{5} = 19.2\\)", "markScheme": ["M1 — attempt to solve simultaneously: \\(-x^4+3x^2+4\\,(=0)\\) (allow one term error)", "M1 dep — attempt to solve their 3-term quadratic in \\(x^2\\): \\((x^2-4)(x^2+1) = 0\\) oe; A1 — \\(x = 2\\) and \\(-2\\) only", "M1 — attempt to integrate their quadratic−quartic (or the other way round) with one term correctly integrated; allow without limits", "A1 — \\(\\left[-\\frac{x^5}{5}+x^3+4x\\right]_{-2}^{2}\\) all correct with correct limits (or limits 0 to 2 then doubled)", "M1 dep — correct substitution of their limits; A1 — \\(\\frac{96}{5}\\) or 19.2"]}, {"id": "s26-p12-q12", "group": "area", "source": {"session": "May/June 2026", "paper": "4037/12", "question": "12"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["14.12", "14.13"], "alsoChapters": [7], "topics": ["area between a line and a curve", "trapezium minus integral", "exact answer with logs"], "marks": 11, "targetMinutes": 17, "difficulty": 3, "statement": "<p>The diagram shows part of the curve \\(\\;y = \\dfrac{10}{4x+1}\\;\\) and the line \\(L\\).</p><p>\\(L\\) passes through the points \\((0, 6)\\) and \\((1.5, 0)\\).<br>The curve and \\(L\\) intersect at the points \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\).</p><p>Find the exact area of the shaded region.&emsp;<b>[11]</b></p>", "figure": {"x": [-0.2, 2], "y": [-1, 7], "xTicks": 0.5, "yTicks": 2, "curves": [{"fn": {"type": "recip", "k": 10, "a": 4, "b": 1}}, {"poly": [-4, 6], "cls": "c1"}], "points": [[0.25, 5, "P"], [1, 2, "Q"], [0, 6, ""], [1.5, 0, ""]]}, "tips": ["<b>Find the line first:</b> through \\((0,6)\\) and \\((1.5,0)\\), gradient \\(= \\dfrac{0-6}{1.5} = -4\\), so \\(y = -4x+6\\).", "Intersections: \\(\\dfrac{10}{4x+1} = -4x+6\\). Multiply up: \\(10 = (-4x+6)(4x+1) \\Rightarrow 16x^2-20x+4 = 0 \\Rightarrow 4x^2-5x+1 = 0 \\Rightarrow (4x-1)(x-1)=0\\), giving \\(x = \\tfrac14, 1\\).", "<b>The shape is line-above-curve</b>, so area = (area under the line) − (area under the curve) between those limits. The line piece is a TRAPEZIUM: \\(\\tfrac12(5+2)\\times\\tfrac34 = \\tfrac{21}{8}\\) (quicker than integrating).", "Curve piece: \\(\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{10}{4x+1}dx = \\dfrac{10}{4}\\ln(4x+1)\\) — divide by the coefficient of \\(x\\). Then \\(\\tfrac{10}{4}\\ln5 - \\tfrac{10}{4}\\ln2 = \\tfrac52\\ln\\tfrac52\\).", "Subtract in the right order (area A0 if reversed): \\(\\tfrac{21}{8} - \\tfrac52\\ln\\tfrac52\\)."], "solution": ["<p>Line \\(L\\): gradient \\(=\\dfrac{0-6}{1.5-0} = -4\\), so \\(y = -4x+6\\).</p>", "<p>Intersections: \\(\\dfrac{10}{4x+1} = -4x+6 \\Rightarrow 10 = -16x^2+20x+6 \\Rightarrow 16x^2-20x+4 = 0\\)</p>", "<p>\\(4x^2-5x+1 = 0 \\Rightarrow (4x-1)(x-1) = 0 \\Rightarrow x = \\tfrac14\\) (P) and \\(x = 1\\) (Q); \\(y = 5\\) and \\(y = 2\\).</p>", "<p><b>Area under the line</b> (trapezium, parallel sides 5 and 2, width \\(1-\\tfrac14 = \\tfrac34\\)): \\(\\tfrac12(5+2)\\times\\tfrac34 = \\dfrac{21}{8}\\).</p>", "<p><b>Area under the curve:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int_{1/4}^{1}\\dfrac{10}{4x+1}dx = \\left[\\dfrac{10}{4}\\ln(4x+1)\\right]_{1/4}^{1} = \\dfrac{10}{4}\\ln5 - \\dfrac{10}{4}\\ln2 = \\dfrac52\\ln\\dfrac52\\).</p>", "<p>Shaded area \\(= \\dfrac{21}{8} - \\left(\\dfrac52\\ln5 - \\dfrac52\\ln2\\right) = \\dfrac{21}{8} - \\dfrac52\\ln\\dfrac52\\).</p>"], "answer": "\\(\\dfrac{21}{8} - \\dfrac52\\ln\\dfrac52\\;\\left(= \\tfrac{21}{8}+\\tfrac52\\ln\\tfrac25\\right)\\)", "markScheme": ["B1 — \\(y = -4x+6\\) oe;  M1 FT — eliminates one unknown, e.g. \\(\\frac{10}{4x+1} = -4x+6\\)", "A1 — \\(0 = -16x^2+20x-4\\) soi (or \\(4x^2-5x+1=0\\));  M1 dep FT — \\(0 = (4x-1)(x-1)\\);  A1 — \\(x = \\frac14\\), \\(x = 1\\)", "B1 — \\(y = 5\\), \\(y = 2\\) or \\([-2x^2+6x]\\) soi", "B1 — area of the trapezium \\(= \\frac{21}{8}\\) (may come from integrating \\([-2x^2+6x]_{1/4}^{1}\\) or from \\(\\frac12(5+2)\\times\\frac34\\))", "2 — \\(\\int\\frac{10}{4x+1}dx = \\frac{10}{4}\\ln(4x+1)\\) (M1 for \\(k\\ln(4x+1)\\), A1 correct)", "M1 dep — correct substitution of their limits in the form \\(k\\ln(4x+1)\\): \\(\\frac{10}{4}\\ln5 - \\frac{10}{4}\\ln2\\)", "A1 — \\(\\frac{21}{8} - \\left(\\frac52\\ln5 - \\frac52\\ln2\\right)\\) oe (A0 if the subtraction is done the wrong way round)"]}, {"id": "w24-p23-q12", "group": "kinematics", "source": {"session": "Oct/Nov 2024", "paper": "4037/23", "question": "12"}, "calc": true, "outcomes": ["14.14"], "alsoChapters": [10], "topics": ["differentiate v to get a", "integrate v to get s", "changes direction when v = 0"], "marks": 9, "targetMinutes": 14, "difficulty": 3, "parts": [{"label": "(a)", "text": "A particle moves in a straight line. Its velocity, v ms⁻¹, at time t seconds is given by \\(v = \\cos t - \\sin t\\). Find the acceleration, a ms⁻², when \\(t = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\).", "marks": 2}, {"label": "(b)", "text": "The displacement of the particle from a fixed point O at time t is s metres. The particle passes through O when t = 0. Find the displacement at the time when the particle first changes direction after passing through O.", "marks": 6}, {"label": "(c)", "text": "Find an expression for a in terms of s.", "marks": 1}], "statement": "<p>A particle moves in a straight line. Its velocity, \\(v\\) ms⁻¹, at time \\(t\\) seconds is given by</p><p style=\"text-align:center\">\\(v = \\cos t - \\sin t\\).</p><p><b>(a)</b> Find the acceleration, \\(a\\) ms⁻², when \\(t = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\).&emsp;<b>[2]</b></p><p>The displacement of the particle from a fixed point \\(O\\) at time \\(t\\) is \\(s\\) metres. The particle passes through \\(O\\) when \\(t = 0\\).</p><p><b>(b)</b> Find the displacement at the time when the particle <b>first changes direction</b> after passing through \\(O\\).&emsp;<b>[6]</b></p><p><b>(c)</b> Find an expression for \\(a\\) in terms of \\(s\\).&emsp;<b>[1]</b></p>", "tips": ["<b>The kinematics chain:</b> \\(s \\xrightarrow{\\text{differentiate}} v \\xrightarrow{\\text{differentiate}} a\\), and back the other way by integrating.", "(a) \\(a = \\dfrac{dv}{dt} = -\\sin t - \\cos t\\). At \\(t=\\pi/3\\) use exact values: \\(-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}-\\tfrac12\\).", "(b) <b>\"Changes direction\" means \\(v = 0\\)</b> — the velocity switches sign. \\(\\cos t = \\sin t \\Rightarrow \\tan t = 1 \\Rightarrow t = \\tfrac{\\pi}{4}\\) (first positive solution).", "(b) Integrate for \\(s\\): \\(s = \\sin t + \\cos t + c\\), and use \"passes through \\(O\\) when \\(t=0\\)\" (so \\(s=0\\)) to get \\(c = -1\\). <b>Do not skip the constant.</b>", "(c) Compare your expressions: \\(a = -(\\sin t + \\cos t)\\) and \\(s = \\sin t + \\cos t - 1\\), so \\(\\sin t + \\cos t = s+1\\) and \\(a = -(s+1)\\)."], "solution": ["<p><b>(a)</b> \\(a = \\dfrac{dv}{dt} = -\\sin t - \\cos t\\).</p>", "<p>At \\(t = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\): \\(a = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2} - \\dfrac12 = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt3+1}{2} \\approx -1.37\\) ms⁻².</p>", "<p><b>(b)</b> Changes direction when \\(v = 0\\): \\(\\cos t = \\sin t \\Rightarrow \\tan t = 1 \\Rightarrow t = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\).</p>", "<p>\\(s = \\displaystyle\\int(\\cos t - \\sin t)dt = \\sin t + \\cos t + c\\).</p>", "<p>At \\(t=0\\), \\(s=0\\): \\(0 + 1 + c = 0 \\Rightarrow c = -1\\), so \\(s = \\sin t + \\cos t - 1\\).</p>", "<p>At \\(t = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\): \\(s = \\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{2}+\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{2} - 1 = \\sqrt2 - 1\\) m.</p>", "<p><b>(c)</b> \\(a = -(\\sin t+\\cos t)\\) and \\(s+1 = \\sin t+\\cos t\\), so \\(a = -(s+1) = -s-1\\).</p>"], "answer": "(a) \\(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt3+1}{2}\\); (b) \\(\\sqrt2-1\\) m; (c) \\(a = -s-1\\)", "markScheme": ["(a) M1 — \\(a = -\\sin t - \\cos t\\); A1 — \\(-\\frac{\\sqrt3+1}{2}\\) oe or awrt \\(-1.37\\)", "(b) M1 — sets \\(v = 0\\): \\(\\cos t = \\sin t\\) or \\(\\tan t = 1\\); A1 — \\(t = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\) oe", "(b) M1 — \\(s = \\int v\\,dt = \\sin t + \\cos t\\,(+c)\\); A1 — correct integration; M1 — uses \\(s=0\\) at \\(t=0\\) to find \\(c = -1\\)", "(b) A1 — \\(\\sqrt2 - 1\\) oe", "(c) B1 — \\(a = -s-1\\) oe"]}, {"id": "s25-p22-q2", "group": "kinematics", "source": {"session": "May/June 2025", "paper": "4037/22", "question": "2"}, "calc": true, "outcomes": ["14.14", "14.15"], "alsoChapters": [4], "topics": ["displacement-time graph", "differentiate a product to get velocity", "factorised form"], "marks": 4, "targetMinutes": 6, "difficulty": 2, "parts": [{"label": "(a)", "text": "A particle P moves in a straight line such that its displacement s from a fixed point O at time t is given by \\(s = (t-4)^2(t-1)\\) for \\(t \\geqslant 0\\). On the axes, sketch the displacement–time graph of P, stating the intercepts with the axes.", "marks": 2}, {"label": "(b)", "text": "Find an expression for the velocity, v, of P. Give your answer in a factorised form.", "marks": 2}], "statement": "<p>In this question, all lengths are in metres and time is in seconds.</p><p>A particle \\(P\\) moves in a straight line such that its displacement \\(s\\) from a fixed point \\(O\\) at time \\(t\\) is given by \\(\\;s = (t-4)^2(t-1)\\;\\) for \\(t \\geqslant 0\\).</p><p><b>(a)</b> On the axes, sketch the displacement–time graph of \\(P\\), stating the intercepts with the axes.&emsp;<b>[2]</b></p><p><b>(b)</b> Find an expression for the velocity, \\(v\\), of \\(P\\). Give your answer in a factorised form.&emsp;<b>[2]</b></p>", "tips": ["(a) This is a cubic in factorised form (Chapter 4 skill): roots at \\(t = 4\\) (<b>repeated</b> — so the curve touches the axis there) and \\(t = 1\\).", "(a) \\(s\\)-intercept at \\(t=0\\): \\((-4)^2(-1) = -16\\). A repeated root means a turning point ON the axis — that shape detail earns the mark.", "(b) \\(v = \\dfrac{ds}{dt}\\). Use the product rule: \\(2(t-4)(t-1) + (t-4)^2\\).", "(b) \"Factorised form\": take out \\((t-4)\\): \\((t-4)\\big[2(t-1)+(t-4)\\big] = (t-4)(3t-6) = 3(t-4)(t-2)\\). Factor the 3 out fully."], "solution": ["<p><b>(a)</b> Roots: \\(t = 1\\) and \\(t = 4\\) (repeated → the curve touches the \\(t\\)-axis at \\(t=4\\)). \\(s\\)-intercept: \\((0-4)^2(0-1) = -16\\).</p>", "<p>Shape: positive cubic rising from \\((0,-16)\\), crossing at \\(t=1\\), a maximum, then touching the axis at \\(t=4\\) before rising.</p>", {"figure": {"x": [-0.3, 5.5], "y": [-18, 8], "xTicks": 1, "yTicks": 5, "curves": [{"poly": [1, -9, 24, -16]}], "points": [[1, 0, "1"], [4, 0, "4"], [0, -16, "(0, −16)"]]}}, "<p><b>(b)</b> \\(v = \\dfrac{ds}{dt} = 2(t-4)(t-1) + (t-4)^2\\)</p>", "<p>\\(= (t-4)\\big[2(t-1) + (t-4)\\big] = (t-4)(3t-6) = 3(t-4)(t-2)\\).</p>"], "answer": "(a) intercepts 1, 4 (touch) and \\(-16\\); (b) \\(v = 3(t-4)(t-2)\\)", "markScheme": ["(a) B1 — correct cubic shape with a maximum in the first quadrant and a point of tangency to the \\(t\\)-axis at the minimum point; must meet the \\(s\\)-axis", "(a) B1 — correct intercepts (1, 4 and \\(-16\\)); must have attempted the correct shape", "(b) M1 — \\(2(t-4)(t-1) + (t-4)^2\\) oe, or expands to \\(s = t^3-9t^2+24t-16\\) AND differentiates to \\(3t^2-18t+24\\)", "(b) A1 — \\(v = 3(t-4)(t-2)\\)"]}]});
